(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["physiology-exam-3-pulmonary-quiz"]={"title":"Pulmonary Physiology I & II — Quiz","category":"Physiology","sem":"summer-1-2026","questions":[{"q":"During quiet (resting) breathing, how is expiration accomplished?","choices":["By active contraction of the diaphragm","Passively, through elastic recoil of the lungs and chest wall","By contraction of the abdominal muscles","By active contraction of the external intercostal muscles"],"answer":1,"exp":"In quiet breathing the inspiratory muscles simply relax and the stretched lung recoils, expelling air passively — no active expiratory effort is needed. Diaphragm and external-intercostal contraction produce inspiration, and the abdominals are recruited only for forced expiration."},{"q":"What happens to pleural (intrapleural) pressure during inspiration?","choices":["It becomes positive","It stays entirely constant throughout the breath","It becomes more negative (about –5 to –8 cm H₂O)","It rises toward zero"],"answer":2,"exp":"As the chest expands, intrapleural pressure falls further below atmospheric (roughly –5 to –8 cm H₂O), pulling the lungs open. It does not turn positive or move toward zero during inspiration — those occur in expiration — and it clearly changes across the breath."},{"q":"How does forced expiration differ from quiet expiration?","choices":["It uses active muscle effort, and pleural pressure can become positive","It relies solely on passive relaxation of the diaphragm muscle","It makes pleural pressure more negative","It requires surfactant to push air out"],"answer":0,"exp":"Forced expiration recruits expiratory muscles (e.g., abdominals), which can drive intrapleural pressure positive to expel air rapidly. Passive relaxation and a more-negative pleural pressure describe quiet breathing and inspiration, and surfactant lowers surface tension rather than expelling air."},{"q":"Lung compliance is best defined as...","choices":["The pressure needed to overcome resistance in the conducting airways","The tendency of the lung to recoil after stretch","The rate of airflow through the bronchioles","The change in lung volume per unit change in pressure (ΔV/ΔP)"],"answer":3,"exp":"Compliance measures how much lung volume changes for a given change in pressure (ΔV/ΔP) — its distensibility. Recoil tendency is elasticity (the opposite idea), while airway resistance and airflow rate describe how easily air moves, not how easily the lung stretches."},{"q":"Something described as “highly elastic,” like lung tissue, will...","choices":["Stretch easily and stay stretched","Resist being stretched and tend to recoil to its original shape","Have very high compliance","Lose its original shape permanently once it has been stretched"],"answer":1,"exp":"Elasticity is the tendency to return to the original shape after stretch, so a highly elastic structure resists deformation and recoils. Stretching easily or staying deformed describes low elasticity / high compliance — the opposite property."},{"q":"Which TWO factors are chiefly responsible for the lung's elastic recoil?","choices":["Airway resistance and dead space","Pulmonary blood flow and lymphatic drainage through the lung","Elastic tissue (collagen/elastin) and alveolar surface tension","Surfactant and mucus production"],"answer":2,"exp":"The lung's recoil comes from its elastic fibers (collagen and elastin) plus the surface tension of the alveolar lining fluid. Resistance, dead space, blood flow, and mucus are unrelated to recoil, and surfactant actually reduces the surface-tension component."},{"q":"How does surface tension at the air–water interface affect the alveoli?","choices":["It draws alveolar walls together and favors collapse","It holds alveoli open by pushing their walls apart","It has no effect on alveolar stability","It increases lung compliance"],"answer":0,"exp":"Surface tension pulls the water-lined alveolar walls inward, tending to collapse the alveolus and lowering compliance. It does not push walls apart or leave stability unaffected — counteracting it is exactly why surfactant is needed."},{"q":"Because airflow varies with r⁴, a small decrease in airway radius will...","choices":["Barely change resistance","Decrease resistance","Only matter within the alveoli themselves, never in the conducting airways","Sharply increase resistance, since radius is raised to the fourth power"],"answer":3,"exp":"With resistance proportional to 1/r⁴, even slight narrowing greatly increases resistance to airflow. It does not decrease or barely change resistance, and the effect is greatest in the airways (the upper airways carry the major resistance), not the alveoli."},{"q":"How does a decrease in lung volume affect airway resistance?","choices":["It increases resistance","It decreases resistance","It eliminates resistance","It has no effect on resistance"],"answer":0,"exp":"As lung volume falls, the airways are less stretched open and narrow, so airway resistance rises. Resistance is never eliminated, and it clearly depends on lung volume, so “no effect” is incorrect."},{"q":"What is the difference between a lung “volume” and a lung “capacity”?","choices":["A capacity is always smaller than a single volume","They are two words for the same thing","A capacity is the sum of two or more lung volumes","A volume can only be measured during exercise"],"answer":2,"exp":"A lung capacity is defined as the sum of two or more lung volumes (e.g., vital capacity = IRV + TV + ERV). Capacities are therefore larger than individual volumes, are not synonymous with them, and are measured at rest."},{"q":"Vital capacity is the sum of which volumes?","choices":["Tidal volume + residual volume","Inspiratory reserve + tidal volume + expiratory reserve","Expiratory reserve + residual volume","Inspiratory reserve volume + the residual volume"],"answer":1,"exp":"Vital capacity — the maximum air expired after a maximal inspiration — equals IRV + TV + ERV. Any combination that includes residual volume is wrong, because residual volume cannot be exhaled and is not part of vital capacity."},{"q":"Functional residual capacity (FRC) represents the...","choices":["Maximum air inspired after a normal expiration","The maximum air expired following a maximal inspiration","Air moved in one normal breath","Air remaining in the lungs after a normal (resting) expiration"],"answer":3,"exp":"FRC (= ERV + RV) is the volume left in the lungs after a normal, resting expiration. The maximum inspired after a normal expiration is inspiratory capacity, the maximum expired after maximal inspiration is vital capacity, and one normal breath is tidal volume."},{"q":"Residual volume is the air that...","choices":["Enters the lungs during a normal breath","Can be forcibly inspired beyond a normal breath","Remains in the lungs even after a maximal expiration","Is exhaled during quiet breathing"],"answer":2,"exp":"Residual volume is the air that cannot be exhaled — it remains even after a maximal expiration, keeping alveoli from collapsing. Normal-breath air is tidal volume, extra inspired air is inspiratory reserve, and quiet-breathing exhaled air is tidal volume."},{"q":"Which lung value CANNOT be measured directly by simple spirometry?","choices":["Tidal volume","Inspiratory reserve volume","Vital capacity","Residual volume"],"answer":3,"exp":"Spirometry measures only air that moves in and out, so it cannot capture residual volume (air that never leaves the lungs) — nor the capacities containing it (FRC, TLC). Tidal volume, inspiratory reserve, and vital capacity are all directly measurable."},{"q":"Inspiratory capacity is the maximum air that can be inspired following...","choices":["A normal (resting) expiration","A maximal expiration","A maximal inspiration","A forced exhalation to residual volume"],"answer":0,"exp":"Inspiratory capacity (= TV + IRV) is the most air inhaled starting from the end of a normal, resting expiration. Starting from a maximal expiration or residual volume would approach total lung capacity, and you cannot inhale further after a maximal inspiration."},{"q":"What is the primary function of pulmonary surfactant?","choices":["To increase surface tension so alveoli stay small","To reduce alveolar surface tension and prevent collapse","To thicken the respiratory membrane for protection","To trap inhaled particles like mucus"],"answer":1,"exp":"Surfactant lowers the surface tension of the alveolar lining fluid, preventing collapse and increasing compliance. It does not raise surface tension, thicken the membrane, or trap particles — those would impair breathing."},{"q":"By Laplace's law (P = 2T/r), at equal surface tension a SMALLER alveolus...","choices":["Requires a smaller pressure to keep from collapsing","Is inherently more stable than a large one","Requires a larger pressure to keep from collapsing","Has no collapse tendency at all"],"answer":2,"exp":"With P = 2T/r, a smaller radius demands a higher pressure to stay open, so small alveoli are inherently less stable — which is why surfactant is essential. The other options reverse or ignore this radius relationship."},{"q":"Without surfactant, what would happen to a small alveolus connected to a larger one?","choices":["The small alveolus would tend to collapse and empty into the larger one","The large alveolus would collapse and empty into the smaller one","Both would remain perfectly stable","Surface tension would disappear"],"answer":0,"exp":"Without surfactant, the higher collapse pressure of the small alveolus (Laplace) drives its air into the larger, lower-pressure alveolus — atelectasis. The reverse flow, perfect stability, and disappearance of surface tension are all incorrect."},{"q":"How does surfactant help stabilize alveoli of different sizes?","choices":["By increasing the surface tension inside the larger alveoli of the lung instead","By enlarging all alveoli equally","By blocking airflow to small alveoli","By reducing surface tension, which lessens the collapse tendency of small alveoli"],"answer":3,"exp":"Surfactant lowers surface tension — most effectively in small alveoli — reducing their collapse tendency and equalizing stability across alveolar sizes. It does not raise tension, resize alveoli uniformly, or block airflow."},{"q":"In what direction does a gas diffuse across the respiratory membrane?","choices":["From lower to higher partial pressure, against the gradient","Down its partial pressure gradient, from higher to lower","Always from blood to alveolus","Independent of any pressure gradient"],"answer":1,"exp":"A gas diffuses passively from higher to lower partial pressure. It cannot move up a gradient without energy, and its direction depends on that gradient (O₂ into blood, CO₂ out) — so “always blood to alveolus” and “independent of gradient” are wrong."},{"q":"According to Fick's law, gas diffusion increases when...","choices":["The membrane is thicker","The surface area is smaller","The partial pressure gradient and surface area are larger","The solubility of the gas in plasma is much lower"],"answer":2,"exp":"Fick's law makes diffusion proportional to the pressure gradient, surface area, and solubility, and inversely proportional to membrane thickness. So a bigger gradient and area speed diffusion, while a thicker membrane, smaller area, or lower solubility slow it."},{"q":"Although CO₂ and O₂ have similar molecular weights, CO₂ diffuses much faster mainly because...","choices":["CO₂ is about 20 times more soluble than O₂","CO₂ has a much larger partial pressure gradient","CO₂ is a much smaller molecule","O₂ cannot cross the membrane at all"],"answer":0,"exp":"CO₂'s roughly 20-fold greater solubility lets it diffuse much faster despite a smaller partial pressure gradient. Its gradient is actually smaller, its molecular weight is similar (not smaller), and O₂ obviously does cross the membrane."},{"q":"How does increasing alveolar ventilation affect alveolar PCO₂?","choices":["It raises alveolar PCO₂","It has no effect on alveolar PCO₂","It raises PCO₂ only during exercise","It lowers alveolar PCO₂"],"answer":3,"exp":"Greater alveolar ventilation washes out CO₂ faster than metabolism produces it, so alveolar (and arterial) PCO₂ falls. Ventilation and PCO₂ are inversely related, so raising PCO₂ or “no effect” is incorrect."},{"q":"Alveolar air is replaced only slowly (a fraction with each breath). What is the physiological benefit?","choices":["It lets CO₂ accumulate to high levels","It keeps alveolar gas concentrations stable, preventing sudden swings","It speeds oxygen loss from the blood","It prevents gas exchange from occurring across the membrane"],"answer":1,"exp":"Because only about one-seventh of alveolar air is exchanged per breath, alveolar O₂ and CO₂ stay relatively constant, buffering blood gases against breath-to-breath swings. It does not let CO₂ build up, speed O₂ loss, or halt exchange."},{"q":"Why is the respiratory (alveolar) membrane extremely thin (~0.2 µm)?","choices":["To increase surface tension","To slow gas diffusion down so it can be regulated","To minimize diffusion distance and speed gas exchange","To store surfactant"],"answer":2,"exp":"At ~0.2 µm thick, the membrane keeps the diffusion distance tiny, allowing rapid gas exchange (Fick's law favors short distances). It is not there to raise surface tension, slow diffusion, or store surfactant."},{"q":"The lungs contain roughly 300 million alveoli. How does this aid gas exchange?","choices":["It provides an enormous surface area for diffusion","It reduces the total surface area","It increases the distance that gas must diffuse","It decreases compliance"],"answer":0,"exp":"Hundreds of millions of alveoli create a vast total surface area, which — per Fick's law — greatly increases gas exchange. This increases (not reduces) surface area and does not lengthen the diffusion distance or lower compliance."},{"q":"Which sequence correctly lists layers a gas crosses in the respiratory membrane, from air to blood?","choices":["Capillary endothelium → basement membranes → alveolar epithelium → surfactant","Interstitial space → surfactant → capillary endothelium","Alveolar epithelium → surfactant → red blood cell","Surfactant/fluid → alveolar epithelium → basement membranes → capillary endothelium"],"answer":3,"exp":"Moving from air to blood, a gas crosses the surfactant/fluid layer, the alveolar epithelium, the fused basement membranes and interstitium, and finally the capillary endothelium. The other sequences scramble this air-to-blood order."},{"q":"If the respiratory membrane becomes thickened, gas exchange is impaired because...","choices":["Surface area increases too much","The diffusion distance increases, slowing gas transfer","The partial pressure gradient across it reverses direction","The solubility of the gases changes"],"answer":1,"exp":"A thicker membrane lengthens the path a gas must diffuse, and by Fick's law greater distance slows transfer (e.g., in edema or fibrosis). Thickening does not add useful surface area, reverse the gradient, or change gas solubility."},{"q":"Respiratory regulation is organized as which three-part system?","choices":["Sensors, a central controller, and effector muscles","Arteries, veins, capillaries, and lymphatics","Alveoli, bronchioles, and pleura","Surfactant, mucus, and cilia"],"answer":0,"exp":"Respiratory control is a feedback loop: sensors (chemoreceptors) gather data, the central controller (brainstem) integrates it, and effectors (respiratory muscles) act. The other trios list vascular, structural, or airway-lining parts, not the control loop."},{"q":"The central chemoreceptors are located in the brainstem and respond mainly to...","choices":["Oxygen levels in arterial blood","Blood pressure changes","CO₂ and H⁺","Lung stretch"],"answer":2,"exp":"The central chemoreceptors sense CO₂ and H⁺ (via H⁺ generated in the CSF). Oxygen sensing is the job of the peripheral chemoreceptors, while blood pressure and lung stretch are detected by other receptors."},{"q":"The peripheral chemoreceptors of the carotid and aortic bodies are especially important for sensing...","choices":["Lung compliance","Airway resistance in the bronchi","Pleural pressure","Low arterial oxygen (PO₂)"],"answer":3,"exp":"The carotid and aortic bodies are the main sensors of low arterial PO₂ (they also respond to CO₂/H⁺). Compliance, resistance, and pleural pressure are mechanical properties, not what these chemoreceptors detect."},{"q":"Increased arterial PCO₂ stimulates the central chemoreceptors indirectly by...","choices":["Binding directly to the peripheral oxygen sensors","Generating H⁺ after CO₂ crosses into the brain/CSF","Reducing blood flow to the medulla","Constricting the airways"],"answer":1,"exp":"CO₂ crosses into the brain/CSF, forms carbonic acid, and releases H⁺; the central chemoreceptors actually respond to that H⁺. They are not oxygen sensors, and CO₂ does not drive breathing by cutting medullary blood flow or narrowing airways."},{"q":"Under normal resting conditions, the most important minute-to-minute stimulus driving ventilation is...","choices":["Arterial PCO₂ (and the resulting H⁺)","A small fall in the arterial PO₂","Blood glucose level","Body temperature"],"answer":0,"exp":"Minute-to-minute ventilation is governed most tightly by arterial PCO₂ and the H⁺ it generates. Modest drops in PO₂ have little effect until PO₂ falls substantially, and glucose and temperature are not the primary respiratory stimulus."},{"q":"When arterial PO₂ falls substantially (PCO₂ held constant), ventilation...","choices":["Decreases","Stays at exactly the same rate as it was before","Increases, driven by the peripheral chemoreceptors","Stops entirely"],"answer":2,"exp":"A substantial fall in PO₂ stimulates the carotid and aortic bodies, increasing ventilation (the hypoxic drive). Ventilation does not fall, stay flat, or stop when oxygen drops."},{"q":"About how much oxygen can each gram of hemoglobin bind?","choices":["0.3 ml O₂","20 ml O₂","100 ml O₂","1.34 ml O₂"],"answer":3,"exp":"Each gram of hemoglobin binds about 1.34 ml of O₂. The 0.3 ml figure is dissolved O₂ per 100 ml of blood, and ~20 ml is the total O₂-carrying capacity per 100 ml — not the per-gram value."},{"q":"Why does hemoglobin matter so much for oxygen transport?","choices":["Hemoglobin dissolves oxygen directly into the plasma, raising dissolved content greatly","Dissolved O₂ alone (~0.3 ml/100 ml) is far too little; Hb raises capacity to ~20 ml/100 ml","Dissolved oxygen carries most of the O₂ in blood","Hemoglobin only carries CO₂, not O₂"],"answer":1,"exp":"Physically dissolved O₂ is tiny (~0.3 ml/100 ml); binding to hemoglobin lifts capacity to about 20 ml/100 ml, so Hb carries the vast majority of oxygen. It does not dissolve O₂ into plasma, and it carries O₂ as well as some CO₂."},{"q":"“Percent saturation” of hemoglobin refers to...","choices":["The absolute amount of O₂ in ml per 100 ml blood","The driving pressure for O₂ diffusion","The fraction of hemoglobin binding sites occupied by O₂","The number of red cells per microliter"],"answer":2,"exp":"Percent saturation is the proportion of hemoglobin binding sites carrying O₂ (a unitless fraction). The absolute amount is “content,” the driving pressure is partial pressure, and red-cell count is a separate measure."},{"q":"The flat upper plateau of the oxygen-hemoglobin dissociation curve means that...","choices":["Hemoglobin stays highly saturated across a wide range of higher PO₂ values","Small drops in PO₂ at high levels cause large drops in saturation","Hemoglobin cannot bind oxygen in the lungs","Saturation and PO₂ are unrelated"],"answer":0,"exp":"On the flat plateau, hemoglobin stays nearly fully saturated even as PO₂ varies over a wide high range — protecting O₂ loading if alveolar PO₂ dips. That is the opposite of large saturation swings, and saturation clearly depends on PO₂."},{"q":"Which change shifts the O₂-hemoglobin dissociation curve to the RIGHT, favoring O₂ unloading?","choices":["Decreased temperature","Increased pH (alkalosis)","Decreased 2,3-BPG","Increased CO₂ and H⁺ (lower pH)"],"answer":3,"exp":"A right shift (lower O₂ affinity, better unloading) results from increased CO₂, increased H⁺/lower pH, higher temperature, and increased 2,3-BPG — the conditions of active tissue. Lower temperature, higher pH, and less 2,3-BPG shift the curve left."},{"q":"Carbon monoxide is dangerous to oxygen transport because it...","choices":["Increases the oxygen-carrying capacity of the blood markedly","Binds hemoglobin far more tightly than O₂, blocking O₂ binding","Cannot bind hemoglobin at all","Only affects dissolved oxygen"],"answer":1,"exp":"Carbon monoxide binds hemoglobin with far greater affinity than oxygen, occupying binding sites and preventing O₂ transport. It reduces (not increases) usable capacity, definitely binds hemoglobin, and acts on hemoglobin rather than dissolved O₂."},{"q":"In what form is MOST CO₂ carried in the blood?","choices":["As dissolved CO₂","Bound to hemoglobin as carbaminohemoglobin","As bicarbonate (HCO₃⁻)","As carbonic acid crystals"],"answer":2,"exp":"About 70% of CO₂ travels as bicarbonate formed in red cells by carbonic anhydrase. Roughly 23% rides on hemoglobin as carbaminohemoglobin and ~7% is dissolved; CO₂ is not carried as solid carbonic-acid crystals."},{"q":"Which enzyme in red cells speeds the conversion of CO₂ and water to carbonic acid?","choices":["Carbonic anhydrase","Carboxypeptidase","Lactate dehydrogenase","ATP synthase"],"answer":0,"exp":"Carbonic anhydrase in red cells rapidly converts CO₂ + H₂O to carbonic acid, which dissociates to H⁺ and bicarbonate. The other enzymes act on peptides, lactate, and ATP production respectively, not on CO₂ hydration."},{"q":"As bicarbonate leaves the red cell in exchange for chloride entering, this exchange is called the...","choices":["Bohr shift","The Haldane shift effect","Sodium shift","Chloride (Hamburger) shift"],"answer":3,"exp":"As bicarbonate leaves the red cell, chloride moves in to preserve electrical neutrality — the chloride (Hamburger) shift. The Bohr and Haldane effects describe how pH/O₂ change gas affinities, and there is no “sodium shift” here."},{"q":"About 23% of CO₂ is transported...","choices":["Dissolved in plasma","Bound to hemoglobin (carbaminohemoglobin)","As free carbonic acid","Inside the circulating white blood cells"],"answer":1,"exp":"Roughly 23% of CO₂ is carried bound to hemoglobin as carbaminohemoglobin. Only ~7% is dissolved, most is bicarbonate, and CO₂ is not stored as free carbonic acid or within white cells."},{"q":"The Haldane effect describes how...","choices":["Low pH increases O₂ unloading","CO₂ increases hemoglobin's O₂ affinity","Binding of O₂ to hemoglobin in the lungs promotes CO₂ release","Chloride ions enter the red blood cell out in the tissues"],"answer":2,"exp":"The Haldane effect: when hemoglobin binds O₂ in the lungs, it releases CO₂ more readily, aiding CO₂ elimination. Low pH increasing O₂ unloading is the Bohr effect, and the other options misstate CO₂'s effect on O₂ affinity and the chloride shift."},{"q":"How does rising blood CO₂ lower blood pH?","choices":["CO₂ combines with water to form carbonic acid, which releases H⁺","CO₂ directly binds and removes bicarbonate","CO₂ destroys the buffering ability of the hemoglobin molecule","CO₂ has no relationship to pH"],"answer":0,"exp":"Dissolved CO₂ forms carbonic acid, which dissociates into H⁺ and bicarbonate, so rising CO₂ raises H⁺ and lowers pH. CO₂ does not remove bicarbonate directly or destroy hemoglobin buffering, and it is tightly linked to pH."},{"q":"If a person hyperventilates and “blows off” CO₂, blood pH will...","choices":["Fall (become more acidic)","Stay exactly the same","Change only if oxygen also falls","Rise (become more alkaline)"],"answer":3,"exp":"Blowing off CO₂ shifts the CO₂ + H₂O ⇌ H⁺ + HCO₃⁻ equilibrium to the left, lowering H⁺ and raising pH (respiratory alkalosis). Losing CO₂ cannot acidify the blood or leave pH unchanged, and the effect does not depend on oxygen."},{"q":"The respiratory system helps regulate blood pH primarily by...","choices":["Producing bicarbonate in the alveoli","Adjusting how much CO₂ is exhaled","Filtering H⁺ out of the blood","Changing hemoglobin concentration"],"answer":1,"exp":"By changing ventilation, the lungs control how much CO₂ is exhaled, shifting the carbonic-acid equilibrium and adjusting pH within minutes. The alveoli don't make bicarbonate or filter H⁺ (a renal function), and hemoglobin level isn't the mechanism."},{"q":"Tissue PO₂ is determined by the balance between...","choices":["Surfactant production and the alveolar surface tension it creates","Alveolar ventilation and dead space","The rate of blood flow (O₂ delivery) and the rate of O₂ consumption","Pleural pressure and compliance"],"answer":2,"exp":"Tissue PO₂ reflects the balance between O₂ delivered by blood flow and O₂ used by the cells — more flow raises it, more consumption lowers it. Surfactant, ventilation/dead space, and pleural mechanics govern the lung, not tissue PO₂."},{"q":"During exercise, more oxygen is released to active muscle largely because...","choices":["Tissue PO₂ falls and local acidity/CO₂ promote O₂ unloading","Hemoglobin permanently loses its ability to bind oxygen","Blood flow to muscle stops","Surfactant increases O₂ release"],"answer":0,"exp":"In exercising muscle, PO₂ drops and the higher CO₂/H⁺ and temperature right-shift the curve (Bohr effect), so hemoglobin releases far more O₂. Hemoglobin doesn't lose function, muscle blood flow rises rather than stops, and surfactant has no role in tissue O₂ release."}]};
